{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792231542,"nonce":"xO93g/4xn2jji5N8rO/yBogICdc=","title":"Site: zylua and zypy pages with CI benchmark tables, and featured languages on the landing page","message":"The site published ZynML's benchmarks only. zylua and zypy had no page, and their numbers came from local runs.\n\n- website/zylua and website/zypy: a description of each runtime in ASD-STE100 style, and a table per architecture of each benchmark on the runtime and its references (LuaJIT and PUC Lua 5.4; PyPy and CPython), by benchmark or iteration time and by whole-process time, with the ratio to LuaJIT or PyPy. Rows whose answer disagrees with the reference are left out, as on the ZynML page.\n- wiki.yml: a lang-bench job per suite (zylua, zypy) on ubuntu-latest and macos-latest. It builds the llvm-backend binary, installs the reference interpreters, runs crates/zyntax_lua/benchmarks/run.py (--runs 5) or crates/zyntax_python/benchmarks/speed/run.py (--fast), adds a meta block (date, commit, os, arch, cpu, cores, RAM, interpreter versions), and uploads the JSON. The aggregate job places it as \u003csuite\u003e/results.json and results-macos.json; the deploy job copies the pages and results.\n- The landing page scrolls past the hero to a Featured Languages section with a card each for ZynML, zylua and zypy.\n- The benchmark page's footer said MIT; the project is Apache 2.0.\n\nThe pages read only what CI publishes: until the workflow runs on main, they say that no results are published yet.","files":null}]}